  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_019, acc=0.639) uses pca_n2000 + resnet50 + avgpool + weighted fusion in a multimodal multiprofile setup. We have strong evidence that avgpool > transformer for aggregation (0.591 vs 0.519 in RNA-only) and weighted > concat for fusion (0.639 vs 0.619), and that pca_n2000 >> scgpt (0.639 vs 0.525). However, scvi_n200 has never been tested in any experiment. Question: Can a learned variational encoder (scvi_n200) match or beat PCA in the multimodal setting, or is the scgpt failure (0.525) indicative that learned RNA encoders are generally inferior to PCA here? Why this is best: config_031 is a direct single-variable comparison to config_019 (only RNA encoder changes from pca_n2000 to scvi_n200), cleanly isolating the encoder effect. Resolving this uncertainty is critical because if scvi_n200 is competitive, it opens a new optimization axis; if not, we can confidently lock in PCA and focus remaining budget on other dimensions. This is more informative than re-testing transformer aggregation (already strongly disfavored) or swapping image encoders (resnet50 vs ViT showed negligible difference)."
}

